\ The `once` cell in the interpreter-free class. Same mechanism as aot_once_cell_image.1z, cut
\ down to what compiles without a quotation-call fallback, so the only thing the run exercises is
\ the cell itself.
\
\ The body reaches a module-level binding and calls another word, so it also proves the freeze
\ walked into the cell and compiled what the body calls rather than only the guard.

runs: ( -- vec ) V{ } ;

(note-run): ( -- ) [ runs 1 #push! drop ] ;

answer: ( -- fixnum ) once [ (note-run) 6 7 * ] ;

read-once: ( -- fixnum ) [ answer ] ;
read-twice: ( -- fixnum ) [ answer answer + ] ;

runs #len .   \ 0  -- nothing runs until the word is referenced
read-once .   \ 42 -- the first read computes
runs #len .   \ 1
read-twice .  \ 84 -- both reads reuse
runs #len .   \ 1  -- one body entry across three reads from two call sites
